+# Checkup
+
+**Checkup** is where FamilyFi tells you what's worth turning on, fixing or updating. The number next to **Checkup** in the menu counts the problems waiting for you.
+
+![The Checkup page](images/checkup.png)
+
+## Recommended protection
+
+Three built-in rules that most households should turn on once their devices are assigned. They're all off on a new install, so nobody loses internet while you're setting up.
+
+| Protection | What it does |
+| --- | --- |
+| **Quarantine** | New devices stay offline until you assign them. |
+| **Lock DNS** | Keeps the people it covers off other DNS servers, so they can't get around website rules. |
+| **Block countries** | Stops devices reaching servers in the countries you list. |
+
+**Turn on all recommended** switches on every one that's off, with its usual groups. **Edit** opens Lock DNS or Block countries to change who it covers. [Built-in rules](Rules-schedules-pause-and-allow#built-in-rules) explains each one in full.
+
+## Things to look at
+
+FamilyFi reads your UniFi settings and devices about once an hour, and lists anything that could get in the way. **Check now** looks again straight away. FamilyFi only reads these settings. The one thing it ever removes is a leftover FamilyFi policy, and only when you ask.
+
+Each item says what FamilyFi saw and why it matters. **How to fix** shows the steps, and some items link to the right page in FamilyFi or to UniFi's own guide. Items come in two kinds:
+
+- **Problems** are worth fixing.
+- **Notes** may be on purpose, such as a guest network FamilyFi doesn't manage. **This is intended** (or **Dismiss**) hides one; **Show again** brings it back.
+
+What Checkup looks for:
+
+- **An old FamilyFi policy is still on your gateway.** A policy with a FamilyFi name that none of your rules use, usually left over from an earlier install. Tick the ones to delete and select **Remove from gateway**, or **Keep it**. FamilyFi never removes one without you asking.
+- **A rule was changed in UniFi.** Someone edited one of FamilyFi's policies in UniFi. FamilyFi changes it back on its own, and the item shows what was different. If changing it back fails, **Put it back** tries again.
+- **Devices use a private Wi-Fi address.** They can come back as new, unassigned devices. See [private Wi-Fi addresses](Family-Things-and-devices#private-wi-fi-addresses).
+- **A network uses its own DNS server.** Website rules might not work there. If that server is your Pi-hole or AdGuard Home, you can ignore this.
+- **Networks FamilyFi doesn't manage.** Devices on them get no rules or quarantine. Guest and work networks are often left out on purpose. Change the list on **Settings → Gateway**.
+- **Sonos, TVs and printers.** Wi-Fi settings that can break Sonos, wired and wireless speakers mixed together, and speakers, TVs or printers your phones can't find because they're on another network.
+- **Smart-home Wi-Fi.** A Wi-Fi network with Things on it that many plugs, bulbs and cameras can't join.
+- **A new FamilyFi release is ready.** **Update** opens **Settings → Maintenance**. See [Updates and backups](Updates-and-backups).
+- **FamilyFi can't follow your update setting.** The `FAMILYFI_UPDATE_TAG` in your `.env` isn't one FamilyFi knows. See [choosing which releases you get](Updates-and-backups#choosing-which-releases-you-get).
